WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.950
Video analysis systems for tactical and technical evaluation. D2.4 Implementation
Guide · Video 4 of 4 · Chapter 5.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:14.545
What academies use video for. Reviewing team tactics, evaluating individual
technique and decisions, and giving players feedback they can see. The hard parts: a

3
00:00:14.595 --> 00:00:18.950
stable camera position, written tag definitions, and feedback that players accept.

4
00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:29.568
Small-academy option. A phone on a tall tripod at the halfway line, plus free video
software, covers all twelve steps. Add the automated camera later, once the routine

5
00:00:29.618 --> 00:00:29.950
works.

6
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:40.191
Before you start. A camera and a tripod as tall as you can safely use. Enough
storage: a full match in HD is several gigabytes. Video software for tagging and

7
00:00:40.241 --> 00:00:43.950
clipping. Consent and information for players and parents.

8
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:46.950
Once, before the first recording.

9
00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:55.950
Step 1. Choose and mark a fixed camera position. As high as safely possible, near
the halfway line, all four corners of the playing area in view.

10
00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:59.950
Figure 5.1  Camera positions. A  Main position, raised, at the halfway line.

11
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.950
Figure 5.1  Camera positions. B  Behind the goal, for finishing or set-piece drills.

12
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:13.627
Step 2. Write a one-line definition for every tag. Example: "Long pass = more than
25 m". Without definitions, two coaches tag the same match differently. Check: Your

13
00:01:13.677 --> 00:01:16.950
tag list fits on one page and every tag has a definition.

14
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:24.950
Step 3. Test agreement between two coders. Two staff tag the same 15-minute segment.
Where they disagree, fix the definition, not the person.

15
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:32.950
Step 4. Set a file naming pattern: date – age group – opponent or session type. A
fixed pattern lets you find a match weeks later.

16
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:40.950
Step 5. Decide who may see footage and where it is stored. Internal analysis and
publication are different uses with different legal bases.

17
00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:43.950
Every recording.

18
00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.950
Step 6. Charge the camera and clear storage the day before.

19
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:55.950
Step 7. Set up at the marked position and check the frame. Check: All four corners
of the playing area are in view before kick-off.

20
00:01:56.000 --> 00:02:00.950
Step 8. Record the whole session or match without stopping. Gaps make timeline tags
useless.

21
00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:08.950
Step 9. Upload or copy the file the same day and name it. A stuck upload is the most
common video problem.

22
00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.950
After every recording.

23
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:17.950
Step 10. Tag the footage using your defined tag list. Add new tags only after
writing a definition.

24
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:26.066
Step 11. Export two or three short clips per player, mostly positive. Players
respond to short, individual, mostly positive clips and switch off during long

25
00:02:26.116 --> 00:02:26.950
critical replays.

26
00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:34.950
Step 12. Review the clips with the player within a few days. Let players pick or
comment on clips. Never use video to punish.

27
00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:38.450
Figure 5.2  A tagging screen. 1  Every tag has a one-line written definition.

28
00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:41.950
Figure 5.2  A tagging screen. 2  Tagged moments on the timeline.

29
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:45.450
Figure 5.2  A tagging screen. 3  Export a few short clips per player.

30
00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:54.713
Interpreting video. Count only what you defined. Repeat the two-coder test once a
season and for every new tagger. Compare the player with their own earlier footage.

31
00:02:54.763 --> 00:03:01.450
Keep the full analysis for staff; the player sees a few clips. Ask the supplier how
each automated statistic is defined.

32
00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:11.035
In training and feedback. Record every match; tag the ones you will use. One
individual review per player per month. Link each clip to a drill in the next

33
00:03:11.085 --> 00:03:16.450
session. Keep group sessions about the team; individual points for one-to-one
meetings.

34
00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:24.829
Common problems. Match video will not upload: Check the title, test upload speed,
use stable Wi-Fi or a cable. Two coaches tag the same match differently: Rewrite the

35
00:03:24.879 --> 00:03:32.450
definition and re-run the agreement test. A parent objects to a clip online: Take
the clip down, then fix the consent routine. All problems: Table 5.1.

36
00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:41.642
Quick start by role. Coach: Pick 2–3 clips per player; mostly positive. Scout:
Compare a player with their own earlier footage. Analyst: Keep camera position, file

37
00:03:41.692 --> 00:03:47.450
naming and tag list fixed. Academy manager: Separate consent for analysis from
consent for publication.

